#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088643} :activity/id #uuid "c7c1a0ce-38f8-537d-a8f0-5c255830b86e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050433 :topic/id #uuid "48b31687-d86d-5039-a373-e5b296daf525" :topic/math-academy-id 879} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088643 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088941 :tutorial/content "When the characteristic equation of a second-order homogeneous ODE has a **repeated root** $\\lambda = c,$ the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x) = Ae^{cx} + Bxe^{cx}.\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, let's solve the following ODE:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 6\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 9y =0.\n$$\n\nAssuming a solution of the form $y=e^{\\lambda x},$ we differentiate and substitute into the ODE:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-6\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+9{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^{\\lambda x}$ is never zero, we can divide by it to get the **characteristic equation**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso $\\lambda = 3$ is a **repeated root**. Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x) = Ae^{3x} + Bxe^{3x}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a4fc9c29-f54a-5346-b7b3-59abdf8bb39f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4049 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0a076b74-a850-5e47-b647-e51c36c1259a" :step/math-academy-id 24226 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088644}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088644 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087772 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087592 :question/id #uuid "81531c55-fe2e-5eff-82ba-9e08abf24a0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6591"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5fb4d6b-2c86-5ad6-898b-5a1cd44e4106" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048391}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088238